{
 "id": "1970-shafer-wildland",
 "name": "Wildland landscape preference ranking – 1960s Utah photographs",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Shafer & Mietz (1970, USDA Forest Service Research Paper NE-162), one of the first quantitative landscape-preference studies. In August 1967, 50 day-users of the Wasatch National Forest near Salt Lake City ranked two sets of seven black-and-white wildland photographs (lakes, streams, waterfalls, mountains, forest) from most to least preferred; the ranks tested the Shafer et al. (1969) landscape-preference model. Here participants rank the same 14 photographs (report Figures 2 and 3, US government work, public domain), shown in random order, by tapping them from most to least liked. File names keep each photo's predicted and observed rank from the report, so new data can be compared directly (rank sums, Kendall's W). Differences from the original study: (1) photos scanned from the printed report instead of 8×10-inch prints; (2) online instead of face-to-face interviews; (3) two short questions about age and outdoor recreation added.",
 "author": "Shafer & Mietz",
 "year": "1970",
 "category": "Academic Research",
